-- title:
--   Cyclic torsor gradings: Q-torsion degrees give unit Q-th powers
-- statement:
--   Let $\Lambda$ be an additive commutative group, let $R$ and $T$ be commutative rings with $T$ an $R$-algebra whose structure map $R \to T$ is injective, and let $L : \Lambda \to$ ($R$-submodules of $T$) satisfy: $L_0$ is the $R$-span of $1 \in T$; every $L_j$ is the $R$-span of a single element of $T$; $x \in L_i$ and $y \in L_j$ imply $xy \in L_{i+j}$; and, conversely, $L_{i+j}$ is contained in the $R$-span of the set of products $xy$ with $x \in L_i$, $y \in L_j$. Let $g \in \Lambda$ and $Q \in \mathbb{N}$ with $Q \cdot g = 0$ (the $\mathbb{N}$-action on $\Lambda$). The assertion is that there exist $t \in T$ and $u \in R$ such that $u$ is a unit of $R$, $L_g$ is the $R$-span of $t$, $t^Q$ equals the image of $u$ in $T$, and $L_{j \cdot g}$ is the $R$-span of $t^j$ for every natural number $j$. Note that the grading is not assumed to be a direct sum decomposition of $T$, and no compatibility beyond the two displayed inclusions is required.
--
--   This is the purely algebraic content of the statement that a $\mu_Q$-torsor over a base with cyclic isotypic pieces has the Kummer shape $\operatorname{Spec} R[t]/(t^Q - u)$ with $u \in R^\times$: the degree-$g$ line is generated by an element whose $Q$-th power is a unit of the base, and the powers of that generator generate the lines in the degrees $j \cdot g$. It is used in the analysis of multiplicative-type pieces of finite flat group schemes, through [`KummerO.exists_units_of_block`](thm.html#KummerO.exists_units_of_block) and [`KummerO.exists_units_of_block_of_isAlgClosed`](thm.html#KummerO.exists_units_of_block_of_isAlgClosed).
